```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

This vignette shows you how to control a chat with a model from an R
script. You pick the format of a request, set options that change how the
model answers, and continue a conversation. You also read the whole reply
of the server, and you catch errors, so that one failed call does not stop
your script.

`vignette("getting-started")` covers what comes first: installing
LM Studio, starting the server, and downloading `google/gemma-3-1b`.

## Start the server and load the model

The server is the program of LM Studio that answers the requests of R.
Start it, and load the model into memory.

```{r start}
library(rlmstudio)

model <- "google/gemma-3-1b"

# Start the server, and wait up to about 30 seconds for it to answer
lms_server_start(wait = 30)

# Load the model
lms_load(model)
```

## Three routes

LM Studio answers chat requests in more than one format. In this vignette,
a route is one of these formats. The `api_type` argument of `lms_chat()`
picks the route. The routes differ in what they support:

| `api_type` | Continue by response id | Log probabilities | `schema` and `ttl` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `"openresponses"`, the default | Yes | Yes | No |
| `"openai"` | No | No | Yes |
| `"native"` | Yes | No | No |

A later section explains the response id. Log probabilities and `schema`
are in `vignette("text-analysis")`. `ttl` sets how long a model stays
loaded with no request, and `?lms_chat` explains it.

Here is the same prompt on each route. A prompt is the text that you send
to the model.

```{r routes}
prompt <- "Name one color. Answer with one word."

# The default route
lms_chat(model, prompt)

# The OpenAI route
lms_chat(model, prompt, api_type = "openai")

# The native route of LM Studio
lms_chat(model, prompt, api_type = "native")
```

Each call returns the text of the reply. On the default route and the
native route, the text also carries a `response_id` attribute.

## Request options

A request option is a field of the request that changes how the model
answers. `lms_chat()` has no argument for most of them. You add each one
to the call by its name, and `lms_chat()` sends it in the request as you
wrote it.

The model writes its reply in tokens. A token is a short piece of text,
such as a word or a part of a word. The `temperature` option sets how much
chance goes into the choice of each token. At 0, the model takes its most
likely token each time.

```{r temperature}
# Set the temperature to 0
lms_chat(model, prompt, temperature = 0)
```

The package does not check the names of request options. The server
checks them, and the routes do not check them in the same way. On the
default route, the server ignores a name that it does not know. The call
below misspells `temperature`, and it still returns a reply with no error.

```{r misspelled}
# "temprature" is not an option that the server knows
lms_chat(model, prompt, temprature = 0)
```

So a misspelled option can go unnoticed. The section "The raw reply" shows
how to see the temperature that the server used. The section "Errors in a
script" shows what the native route does with the same call.

## A follow-up question

A response id is a name that the server gives to a reply that it keeps.
On the default route and the native route, the text of a reply carries its
response id in the `response_id` attribute. Pass that id as
`previous_response_id` in your next call, and the server sends the earlier
prompt and reply to the model with your new prompt.

Each call below sets `temperature = 0`, so that the model takes its most
likely answer.

```{r follow-up}
# Tell the model a fact
first <- lms_chat(
  model,
  "My favorite color is green. Reply with OK.",
  temperature = 0
)
first

# Ask about the fact in a follow-up
lms_chat(
  model,
  "What is my favorite color? Answer with one word.",
  previous_response_id = attr(first, "response_id"),
  temperature = 0
)

# Ask the same question with no response id
lms_chat(
  model,
  "What is my favorite color? Answer with one word.",
  temperature = 0
)
```

With the response id, the model answered from the first prompt. With no
response id, the model did not see the first prompt, and its answer was a
guess.

The OpenAI route has no response id. With `api_type = "openai"`,
`lms_chat()` stops with an error if you give `previous_response_id`.

## A whole conversation

Conversation history is the list of the earlier messages of a chat. On the
OpenAI route, you send the whole history with each request.
`lms_chat_openai()` takes it in its `messages` argument, as a data frame
with one row for each message. The `role` column says who wrote the
message, and the `content` column holds the text. The roles are:

- `system`, for the system prompt, a set of instructions for the model.
- `user`, for your prompts.
- `assistant`, for the replies of the model.

You write the history yourself, so an `assistant` row can hold a reply that
the model never gave.

```{r history}
history <- data.frame(
  role = c("system", "user", "assistant", "user"),
  content = c(
    "You answer in one short sentence.",
    "My favorite color is green.",
    "Green is a nice color.",
    "What is my favorite color?"
  )
)
history

# Send the whole conversation
reply <- lms_chat_openai(model, messages = history, temperature = 0)
reply
```

To continue the conversation, add the reply and your next prompt to the
data frame as new rows, and send it again. `trimws()` removes the line
break at the end of the reply.

```{r history-next}
# Add the reply and a new prompt as two rows
history <- rbind(
  history,
  data.frame(
    role = c("assistant", "user"),
    content = c(trimws(reply), "Name a fruit of that color.")
  )
)

# Send the longer conversation
lms_chat_openai(model, messages = history, temperature = 0)
```

## The raw reply

The server sends its reply in JSON, a text format for data. The raw reply
is that whole reply, read into R as a list. With `simplify = FALSE`,
`lms_chat()` returns the raw reply in place of the text.

```{r raw}
raw <- lms_chat(model, prompt, temperature = 0, simplify = FALSE)

# The fields of the raw reply
names(raw)

# The temperature that the server used
raw$temperature
```

The raw reply holds more than the text, such as the `temperature` that the
server used. Here is the call with the misspelled option from the section
"Request options".

```{r raw-misspelled}
raw_typo <- lms_chat(model, prompt, temprature = 0, simplify = FALSE)

# The temperature that the server used
raw_typo$temperature
```

The server did not use a temperature of 0. It ignored `temprature` and
used a temperature that the call did not set.

## Errors in a script

When a call fails, R creates a condition, an object that describes the
error. A condition class is a name that says what kind of error a
condition is. `tryCatch()` can run different code for each condition
class. The package uses these classes for a failed chat:

- `rlmstudio_no_server`: no server answers at the address in `host`.
- `rlmstudio_api_error`: the server answered with an error. The `status`
  field of the condition holds the HTTP status, and its `code` field holds
  the error code that the server gave.
- `rlmstudio_model_mismatch`: the reply came from a model other than the
  one that you asked for. The `model` field holds the model that you asked
  for, and the `reply_model` field holds the model that answered.

The function below calls `lms_chat()`. If the call fails with one of these
classes, the function prints a message and returns `NA`. In a loop over
many prompts, such a function keeps one failed call from stopping the
loop.

```{r chat-or-na}
# Chat, and return NA with a message if the call fails
chat_or_na <- function(...) {
  tryCatch(
    lms_chat(...),
    rlmstudio_no_server = function(cnd) {
      message("No server answered.")
      NA_character_
    },
    rlmstudio_api_error = function(cnd) {
      message("The server refused the call: status ", cnd$status,
              ", code ", cnd$code, ".")
      NA_character_
    },
    rlmstudio_model_mismatch = function(cnd) {
      message("The reply came from ", cnd$reply_model,
              ", not from ", cnd$model, ".")
      NA_character_
    }
  )
}
```

The first call below goes to port 1, where no server runs. The other calls
misspell the model name or an option.

```{r errors}
# No server listens at this address
chat_or_na(model, prompt, host = "http://localhost:1")

# A misspelled model name on the native route
chat_or_na("google/gemma-3-1bb", prompt, api_type = "native")

# The same misspelled model name on the default route
chat_or_na("google/gemma-3-1bb", prompt)

# A misspelled option on the native route
chat_or_na(model, prompt, api_type = "native", temprature = 0)
```

The native route refused the misspelled model name and the misspelled
option. On the default route, the server did not refuse the misspelled
model name. It answered with the model that was loaded, and the package
raised `rlmstudio_model_mismatch` when it read the reply.

## Clean up

When you are done, unload the model to free its memory, and stop the
server.

```{r clean-up}
# Remove the model from memory
lms_unload(model)

# Stop the local server
lms_server_stop()
```
